🚨 **BLOCKING (new, observed red): macOS-only `security` CLI invoked with no platform guard — `ci_profile_serial_precheck_test.dart` fails on Linux (Core tests, shard 0/4)**

The rewritten suite drives the real `scripts/ci-sign doctor` end-to-end, which is great — but `setUpAll`/`tearDownAll` shell out to the macOS-only `security` binary unconditionally:

```dart
final create = Process.runSync('security', [
  'create-keychain', '-p', 'fixture-pass', kcPath,
]);
expect(create.exitCode, 0, reason: create.stderr);
```

There is no `Platform.isMacOS` guard or `skip` anywhere in the file, so on the Linux CI runners the whole suite dies before any test runs:

```
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6259084Z ##[group]❌ test/ci_profile_serial_precheck_test.dart: (setUpAll) (failed)
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6261502Z ProcessException: No such file or directory
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6261992Z   Command: security create-keychain -p fixture-pass /tmp/ci_sign_precheckQLDPPH/fixture.keychain-db
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6267588Z dart:io                                           Process.runSync
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6268499Z test/ci_profile_serial_precheck_test.dart 107:28  main.<fn>
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6618538Z ##[group]❌ test/ci_profile_serial_precheck_test.dart: (tearDownAll) (failed)
2026-10-28T17:15:48.6619406Z   Command: security delete-keychain /tmp/ci_sign_precheckQLDPPH/fixture.keychain-db
2026-10-28T17:19:53.7551042Z ##[error]3162 tests passed, 2 failed, 1 skipped.
```

(run 40732738245, job 122974266152 — the only failures in shard 0/4, and both are the setUp/tearDown of this file.)

**Fix** — gate the keychain-backed teeth to macOS the way `test/cube/backends/macos_sandbox_live_test.dart` does:

```dart
const isMacOS = Platform.isMacOS;

void main() {
  if (!isMacOS) {
    // The fixture keychain (`security create-keychain`) only exists on macOS;
    // the plistlib/openssl halves of `doctor` are covered on every platform
    // by the pure-parsing assertions below.
  }
  ...
}
```

— either `test(..., skip: !Platform.isMacOS ...)` per test, or an early `return` from `main()` with a loud skip reason naming `security`. The openssl cms/plist fixtures themselves are platform-neutral; only the fixture **keychain** (lines ~107–121 and ~124) is macOS-bound, so a split keeps the plist-encoding coverage green on Linux while the keychain teeth stay macOS-only.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `ci-sign install` — the highest-risk surface — has no behavioral test** *(re-post of open thread `PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6xN2_Y` — unchanged on this head)*

`ci_profile_serial_precheck_test.dart` sets a great precedent: it runs the **real** `scripts/ci-sign doctor` against a sandbox keychain (`CI_SIGN_KEYCHAIN` + fixture p12) covering every plist encoding plus both failure teeth. `install` — the command that mutates persistent host state (create/import/rotation/partition-list/manifest write) — only gets static string asserts here (AC2). A same-shaped behavioral suite would pin the teeth that actually caused the incident chain:

1. **idempotency**: two `install <label>` runs with the same fixture p12 → second is a no-op (`identity … is up to date`), manifest unchanged;
2. **rotation**: install p12-A, then p12-B for the same label → A's identity is gone from the keychain (delete-identity path), manifest carries B's sha1;
3. **invalid p12 / wrong password** → exit 1 with the "cannot read P12" error, manifest not written, keychain untouched;
4. **verify teeth**: corrupt the manifest's `sha1=` line → `ci-sign sha1 <label>` dies with "corrupt manifest" instead of silently falling through to a same-named identity (the AC4 invariant, exercised for real);
5. **lock contention** (optional): hold `$CFG/install.lock` → install waits, then dies with "holds the lock" after the 120 s budget.

All of it already works on the `CI_SIGN_KEYCHAIN` override the doctor tests use — no macOS-host assumptions beyond what the precheck suite already makes (once that suite is platform-gated per the blocking review comment).

